research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:355688:+{"id":"rem-strength-order-of-the-nonnegative-tests","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.csudh.edu/~pong/a3/Notes_01.html","https://people.math.sc.edu/girardi/m5545/TBB-AllChapters-Landscape.pdf","https://people.math.binghamton.edu/dikran/478/Ch5.pdf","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convergence_tests","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abel%E2%80%93Dini%E2%80%93Pringsheim_theorem"],"rationale":"The source set establishes the ratio-root strength order, Kummer specializations, Raabe and Gauss criteria, and the Abel–Dini no-final-comparison obstruction. The item is a repository-specific synthesis of those results and local availability constraints. It also contains one inaccurate alteration about a 1/(k log k) Gauss remainder, recorded as an A2 falsehood; as a Remark it has no separate proof component.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-355689-+{"id":"fs-nth-term-test-converse","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter places the divergent harmonic series immediately after the nth-term condition precisely to show that terms tending to zero are not sufficient. The item turns that standard failed converse into an explicit false statement and refutes it with the same family under the local index convention. The numbered refutation is locally written.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-355690-+{"id":"fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convergence_tests","https://math.csudh.edu/~pong/a3/Notes_01.html"],"rationale":"The sourced ratio test requires liminf>1 for divergence, while the CSUDH notes give oscillating-ratio geometric witnesses showing why limsup cannot replace it. The item formulates the pedagogical false alteration and uses a closely related bounded oscillating multiplier. The detailed computation is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-355691-+{"id":"fs-universal-comparison-series","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abel%E2%80%93Dini%E2%80%93Pringsheim_theorem"],"rationale":"The Abel–Dini–Pringsheim theorem explicitly constructs, from any divergent positive series, another divergent positive series with smaller terms; this rules out a slowest divergent series and any single universal comparison series. The item presents the negation as a false claim and follows the sourced construction b_n/B_n with local quantifiers made explicit.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-355692-+{"id":"ex-harmonic-series-diverges","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Example 4.11 states that the harmonic series diverges and gives the same powers-of-two block argument and logarithmic-rate lower bounds. The item adds a condensation proof and formalises the explicit lower bound in local finite-sum notation; its Oresme block proof follows the source.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-355693-+{"id":"ex-sum-of-reciprocal-squares-converges","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Example 4.20 proves convergence of sum 1/n^2 by the same comparison 1/(n+1)^2<1/(n(n+1)) and obtains the upper bound 2. The item shifts that calculation to k>=2 and states the safe non-strict bound at most 2. Its verification follows the sourced telescoping comparison.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
